$/={mechanism_details:[{part1:"Gene therapy treats disease by delivering a therapeutic gene, or by replacing, supplementing, or silencing a faulty gene; it does not necessarily cut the genome at a specific DNA sequence.",source:"[2][14]"},{part2:"CRISPR gene editing uses a guide RNA to bring a Cas nuclease to a chosen DNA site, where it creates a double-strand break; repair by NHEJ often disrupts a gene, while HDR can make a precise sequence change.",source:"[2][14]"}],delivery_details:[{part1:"Gene therapy commonly uses viral vectors, especially adeno-associated virus and lentiviral systems, because they efficiently carry therapeutic DNA into cells.",source:""},{part2:"This delivery approach can cause immunogenic toxicity and, with integrating vectors, insertional oncogenesis.",source:""},{part3:"CRISPR can be delivered by viral vectors, lipid nanoparticles, or by direct delivery of Cas protein and guide RNA as a ribonucleoprotein complex; the core challenge is getting the editing machinery into the right cells long enough to act."}]}
